<p>
    90 minutes, 4 questions, 25 points, 45% of your score. Every point is
    earned by writing code against a specification. The four questions are
    always the same four types, in the same order. Meet each one before you
    meet it on exam day.
</p>

<table>
    <tr><th>#</th><th>Type</th><th>Points</th><th>Wants from you</th><th>Our training</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td><a href="methods-and-control.html">Methods and Control Structures</a></td><td>7</td>
        <td>Two methods or a constructor + method, given specs and examples. Part A: iteration and conditionals. Part B: String methods.</td>
        <td>E1 (Jan 5), loops and if labs all fall</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td><a href="class-design.html">Class Design</a></td><td>7</td>
        <td>A whole class from a spec table. Fields, constructor, methods, toString sometimes.</td>
        <td>5.6a, E2 (Jan 29), Unit 5 all month</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td><a href="data-analysis.html">Data Analysis with ArrayList</a></td><td>5</td>
        <td>Algorithm work over an ArrayList of objects: filter, count, total, find-max. No arrays on this question anymore.</td>
        <td>6.6, 7.5, E3 (Mar 15)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td><a href="two-d-array.html">2D Array</a></td><td>6</td>
        <td>Grid traversal and processing: row/column walks, neighbors, bounds.</td>
        <td>Unit 8, 8.6, E4 (Apr 1)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The Universal Rubric Truths</h2>

<p>Read these once a month from January on. They are how points are actually
given and taken:</p>

<ol>
    <li><strong>You may use constructs the question did not "teach."</strong>
    If you know a loop, use a loop. The rubric grades behavior, not style.</li>
    <li><strong>Answers must be in the given format.</strong> Method headers
    are provided or specified: copy them exactly. Wrong name or wrong
    signature can zero a whole part.</li>
    <li><strong>No penalty for helper methods you define and use</strong>,
    as long as they work and the required method exists as specified.</li>
    <li><strong>Compilation errors kill.</strong> Unclosed braces, missing
    semicolons, undeclared variables: if it would not compile, that part
    scores zero even if the logic was right. Neat code is scoring code.</li>
    <li><strong>Partial credit is generous, in both directions.</strong> A
    correct loop body with a broken boundary may still earn the algorithm
    point. A perfect answer crossing out earns nothing. Never leave a part
    blank: attempt structure earns structure points.</li>
    <li><strong>Java Quick Reference is in your packet.</strong> Check
    signatures against it before moving on. set returns the OLD element.
    remove returns the REMOVED element. Examine those rows until they are
    reflexes.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not re-implement the given class.</strong> FRQ 3 and 4
    provide classes (like an Item or a Grid). Call their methods. The rubric
    expects calls, not rewrites.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Time Plan (90 minutes, 25 points)</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Minutes</th><th>Move</th></tr>
    <tr><td>0-5</td><td>Read all four questions. Start with your strongest
        type. Confidence compounds; panic compounds too.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5-25</td><td>Question 1 (7 pts, two parts). Budget 20 min.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>25-45</td><td>Question 2 (7 pts). Budget 20 min. Watch the
        clock hardest here: class design expands to fill all available time.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>45-60</td><td>Question 3 (5 pts). Budget 15 min.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>60-75</td><td>Question 4 (6 pts). Budget 15 min.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>75-90</td><td>Sweep: every blank part gets an attempt. Check
        braces, semicolons, headers. Compilation pass beats one more clever
        line.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice Sets</h2>

<p>Eight full RPG-format FRQs, two per type. Round A pairs are for the spring
    FRQ days and clinics; Round B is the mock and after. Each includes the
    question, a solution with commentary, and the rubric for self-scoring.</p>

<table>
